    "turn_text": "Well, the first thing is to know what's going on and to understand what is at stake here. It's what is nominally called the rule of nations in which one nation invading another is simply not acceptable since World War II in the establishment of the United Nations. Second is the democracy. If Putin is able to get away with this, certainly the Ukrainian democracy disappears. and autocrats in other parts of the world will be emboldened because they say, well, a Putin can do it, we could do it in our country or we could do it in the neighboring country. And so that's important. Secondly, America's values are free people in a democratic process deciding what they want to do. And so if the Americans If the American values of government and democracy and freedom are of value to us, they are a value to the rest of the world, the populations wherever they may be, not only because those people will be better off and they'll be able to decide for themselves and literally be free of an autocracy and secret police and the like, but also The more democracies there are around the world, the safer the United States is. The stronger our alliances, the interconnection between democracies builds safety for America. And so for all of us, we should be willing to sacrifice a slightly higher price at the pump or even a significantly higher price at the pump. so that the Ukrainian people are able to fight back. And so that Putin has weakened his economic situation, has weakened by those sanctions, which, when it comes to oil, may very well lead to a higher gas prices. Now, the other part of this is that the American economy is largely built on trade relationships with other democracies. And that needs to be maintained. And so therefore the European Union and the NATO alliance are taken together, our strongest trading partners.",
